{"title":"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Which of the following statements is false?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/6/226f53a9a3cb67a136ae782a59331e0808b37b45.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(A) \\ All\\ equilateral\\ triangles\\ are\\ congruent\\ to\\ each\\ other.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"445\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/d/c9d0a02ba0c15b247c47b529d93fa71bf2a1539a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(B) \\  All\\ equilateral\\ triangles\\ are\\ convex.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"311\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/4/e04f61d640de985d9b070de32ef586e7dcdc6f25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(C) \\  All\\ equilateral\\ triangles\\ are\\ equianguilar.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"354\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/8/c88fc351ff92c4af68ae276b983120524309285d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(D) \\  All\\ equilateral\\ triangles\\ are\\ regular\\ polygons.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"389\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/e/16e178f312df7d4b2b86ffe0499599e21d1d29e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm{(E) \\  All\\ equilateral\\ triangles\\ are\\ similar\\ to\\ each\\ other.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"419\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>An equilateral triangle is isosceles, and we find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/4385a1d07ef592e0f2b822f871231063cc54ee44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A=\\angle B=\\angle C$\" width=\"129\" height=\"13\" /> if we use the property of isosceles triangles that if two sides of a triangle are equal then the opposite angles are equal. Thus equilateral triangles are equiangular, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is true.\n</p><p>Regular polygons are both equilateral and equiangular, and so are equilateral triangles are both equilateral (by definition) and equiangular (by the above argument). Thus equilateral triangles are regular polygons and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is true. \n</p><p>Since all of the angles in an equilateral triangle are congruent, all equilateral triangles are similar by AAA similarity.  Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is true.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/8/4385a1d07ef592e0f2b822f871231063cc54ee44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A=\\angle B=\\angle C$\" width=\"129\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/75992e4660b456d390aa44c5b3edef6ce29a91c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A+\\angle B+\\angle C=180$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"176\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/e/c1ed86a483ac4371dde2ca19ce53bb0ce17e32ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle A=60^{\\circ}$\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />. Since no other angles are above <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f0cd95f95238159d02dbcea0aca632c2928991c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" />, all equilateral triangles are convex.  Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is true.\n</p><p>This just leaves choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/a/29a701b2116abd34276d7c9fe5445d20dccf92ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathrm{(A)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"39\" height=\"29\" />.  This is clearly false:  an equilateral triangle with side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is not congruent to an equilateral triangle with side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME\" title=\"1999 AHSME\">1999 AHSME</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1999 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=44&amp;year=1999\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"1999 AHSME Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1999_AHSME_Problems/Problem_11\" title